---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { supportEmail } from '../lib/site';
---
<SiteLayout title="Privacy Policy — Omnibar for Safari" description="How Omnibar handles current addresses, search text, local preferences, and optional Kagi private session links." section="privacy">
  <div class="document wrap">
    <header class="document-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Privacy policy</p><h1>Your browsing<br />stays <em>your business.</em></h1><p>Omnibar does not collect browsing history, run analytics, or send your addresses or searches to its developer.</p><p class="document-date">Last updated September 13, 2026</p></header>
    <div class="document-body">
      <section><h2>What this policy covers</h2><p>This policy covers Omnibar for Safari on macOS and this product website. Omnibar is an independent, free, open-source Safari extension. Contact its developer at <a href={`mailto:${supportEmail}`}>{supportEmail}</a> with privacy questions.</p></section>

      <section><h2>The current address and search text</h2><p>When you open Omnibar, the extension asks Safari for the current tab’s URL and page properties needed to identify Private Browsing. It holds the current address in memory for the popover session and clears that session when the popover closes.</p><p>Omnibar does not save a browsing-history database, collect page contents, fetch search suggestions, or run analytics. It includes no content scripts or injected styles. Website access is declared because Safari requires it before exposing page properties such as the URL.</p><p>When you submit an address, Safari navigates to that destination. When you submit a search, Safari navigates to the selected search provider with your search text in the URL. The destination website or search provider receives that request and may process information under its own privacy policy. Safari may retain navigation history according to its settings and browsing mode.</p></section>

      <section><h2>Settings stored on your Mac</h2><p>Omnibar saves search-engine order and custom engine names and URL templates in local preferences. It uses your Mac’s Region setting to choose an initial engine order. This does not require location permission or a network lookup.</p><p>These preferences are used to provide the app’s features and are not sent to Omnibar’s developer. You can reorder engines and edit or remove custom engines in Search Settings. Custom templates are ordinary preferences; do not put passwords or private session tokens in them.</p></section>

      <section><h2>Optional Kagi private session link</h2><p>If you choose to save a Kagi private session link in its dedicated settings field, Omnibar stores it separately in the local macOS Keychain. Omnibar does not synchronize the link to iCloud, display it again, or send it to its developer.</p><p>The saved link is used only for Kagi searches when Safari confirms Private Browsing. In that case, the token is included in the navigation request to Kagi, allowing Kagi to identify your session. Direct address navigation and other search engines do not read this credential. If Safari does not provide the required page properties, Omnibar uses the regular Kagi URL.</p><p>You can delete the stored link with <strong>Remove Link</strong> in <strong>Search Settings → Kagi Private Browsing</strong>. Kagi controls the linked account and session. Its <a href="https://help.kagi.com/kagi/privacy/private-browser-sessions.html">session-link documentation</a> explains how to invalidate a session.</p></section>

      <section><h2>Support messages</h2><p>If you email support, the developer receives your email address and the information you choose to send, and uses that information to respond to your request. Do not send passwords, Kagi session links, or sensitive browsing details.</p></section>

      <section><h2>This website</h2><p>This is a static website hosted on GitHub Pages. The site adds no analytics, tracking pixels, advertising, externally hosted fonts, or client-side tracking scripts.</p><p>GitHub, as the hosting provider, may process technical information about your visit. GitHub states that visitors’ IP addresses are logged for security purposes. See <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pages/getting-started-with-github-pages/about-github-pages#data-collection">GitHub Pages data collection</a> and <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub’s Privacy Statement</a> for details.</p></section>

      <section><h2>Changes and contact</h2><p>If Omnibar’s data handling changes, this policy will be updated and the date above will change. For questions about this policy, contact <a href={`mailto:${supportEmail}`}>{supportEmail}</a>.</p></section>
    </div>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
